.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt OPENSSL 1ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm openssl
.Nd OpenSSL command line program
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm openssl
.Em command
.Oo
.Em options
\&...
.Oc
.Oo
.Em parameters
\&...
.Oc
.Pp
.Nm openssl
.Ar no- Ns Em XXX
.Oo
.Em options
.Oc
.Pp
.Nm openssl
.Fl help
|
.Fl version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
OpenSSL is a cryptography toolkit implementing the Secure Sockets Layer
(SSL) and Transport Layer Security (TLS) network protocols and related
cryptography standards required by them.
.Pp
The
.Sy openssl
program is a command line program for using the various cryptography
functions of OpenSSL's
.Sy crypto
library from the shell.
It can be used for
.Bd -literal
 o  Creation and management of private keys, public keys and parameters
 o  Public key cryptographic operations
 o  Creation of X.509 certificates, CSRs and CRLs
 o  Calculation of Message Digests and Message Authentication Codes
 o  Encryption and Decryption with Ciphers
 o  SSL/TLS Client and Server Tests
 o  Handling of S/MIME signed or encrypted mail
 o  Timestamp requests, generation and verification
.Ed
.Sh COMMAND SUMMARY
The
.Sy openssl
program provides a rich variety of commands
.Pf ( Em command
in the
.Sx SYNOPSIS
above).
Each command can have many options and argument parameters, shown above
as
.Em options
and
.Em parameters .
.Pp
Detailed documentation and use cases for most standard subcommands are
available (e.g.,
.Xr openssl-x509 1 Ns ).
The subcommand
.Xr openssl-list 1
may be used to list subcommands.
.Pp
The command
.Sy no- Ns Em XXX
tests whether a command of the specified name is available.
If no command named
.Em XXX
exists, it returns 0 (success) and prints
.Sy no- Ns Em XXX ;
otherwise it returns 1 and prints
.Em XXX .
In both cases, the output goes to
.Sy stdout
and nothing is printed to
.Sy stderr .
Additional command line arguments are always ignored.
Since for each cipher there is a command of the same name, this provides
an easy way for shell scripts to test for the availability of ciphers in
the
.Sy openssl
program.
.Pf ( Sy no- Ns Em XXX
is not able to detect pseudo-commands such as
.Sy quit ,
.Sy list ,
or
.Sy no- Ns Em XXX
itself.)
.Ss Configuration Option
Many commands use an external configuration file for some or all of
their arguments and have a
.Sy -config
option to specify that file.
The default name of the file is
.Pa openssl.cnf
in the default certificate storage area, which can be determined from
the
.Xr openssl-version 1
command using the
.Sy -d
or
.Sy -a
option.
The environment variable
.Sy OPENSSL_CONF
can be used to specify a different file location or to disable loading a
configuration (using the empty string).
.Pp
Among others, the configuration file can be used to load modules and to
specify parameters for generating certificates and random numbers.
See
.Xr config 5
for details.
