.Sh OPTIONS
Details of which options are available depend on the specific command.
This section describes some common options with common behavior.
.Ss Program Options
These options can be specified without a command specified to get help
or version information.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -help
Provides a terse summary of all options.
For more detailed information, each command supports a
.Sy -help
option.
Accepts
.Sy --help
as well.
.It Sy -version
Provides a terse summary of the
.Sy openssl
program version.
For more detailed information see
.Xr openssl-version 1 .
Accepts
.Sy --version
as well.
.El
.Ss Common Options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -help
If an option takes an argument, the "type" of argument is also given.
.It Sy --
This terminates the list of options.
It is mostly useful if any filename parameters start with a minus sign:
.Bd -literal
 openssl verify [flags...] -- -cert1.pem...
.Ed
.El
.Ss Format Options
See
.Xr openssl-format-options 1
for manual page.
.Ss Pass Phrase Options
See the
.Xr openssl-passphrase-options 1
manual page.
.Ss Random State Options
Prior to OpenSSL 1.1.1, it was common for applications to store
information about the state of the random-number generator in a file
that was loaded at startup and rewritten upon exit.
On modern operating systems, this is generally no longer necessary as
OpenSSL will seed itself from a trusted entropy source provided by the
operating system.
These flags are still supported for special platforms or circumstances
that might require them.
.Pp
It is generally an error to use the same seed file more than once and
every use of
.Sy -rand
should be paired with
.Sy -writerand .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -rand Em files
A file or files containing random data used to seed the random number
generator.
Multiple files can be specified separated by an OS-dependent character.
The separator is
.Qo Li ; Qc
for MS-Windows,
.Qo Li , Qc
for OpenVMS, and
.Qo Li : Qc
for all others.
Another way to specify multiple files is to repeat this flag with
different filenames.
.It Sy -writerand Em file
Writes the seed data to the specified
.Em file
upon exit.
This file can be used in a subsequent command invocation.
.El
.Ss Certificate Verification Options
See the
.Xr openssl-verification-options 1
manual page.
.Ss Name Format Options
See the
.Xr openssl-namedisplay-options 1
manual page.
.Ss TLS Version Options
Several commands use SSL, TLS, or DTLS. By default, the commands use TLS
and clients will offer the lowest and highest protocol version they
support, and servers will pick the highest version that the client
offers that is also supported by the server.
.Pp
The options below can be used to limit which protocol versions are used,
and whether TCP (SSL and TLS) or UDP (DTLS) is used.
Note that not all protocols and flags may be available, depending on how
OpenSSL was built.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -ssl3 Ns , Sy -tls1 Ns , Sy -tls1_1 Ns , Sy -tls1_2 Ns , Sy -tls1_3 Ns , Sy -no_ssl3 Ns , Sy -no_tls1 Ns , Sy -no_tls1_1 Ns , Sy -no_tls1_2 Ns , Sy -no_tls1_3
These options require or disable the use of the specified SSL or TLS
protocols.
When a specific TLS version is required, only that version will be
offered or accepted.
Only one specific protocol can be given and it cannot be combined with
any of the
.Sy no_
options.
The
.Sy no_*
options do not work with
.Sy s_time
and
.Sy ciphers
commands but work with
.Sy s_client
and
.Sy s_server
commands.
.It Sy -dtls Ns , Sy -dtls1 Ns , Sy -dtls1_2
These options specify to use DTLS instead of TLS. With
.Sy -dtls ,
clients will negotiate any supported DTLS protocol version.
Use the
.Sy -dtls1
or
.Sy -dtls1_2
options to support only DTLS1.0 or DTLS1.2, respectively.
.El
.Ss Engine Options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -engine Em id
Load the engine identified by
.Em id
and use all the methods it implements (algorithms, key storage, etc.),
unless specified otherwise in the command-specific documentation or it
is configured to do so, as described in
.Xr config 5 .
.Pp
The engine will be used for key ids specified with
.Sy -key
and similar options when an option like
.Sy -keyform engine
is given.
.Pp
A special case is the
.Qo Li loader_attic Qc
engine, which is meant just for internal OpenSSL testing purposes and
supports loading keys, parameters, certificates, and CRLs from files.
When this engine is used, files with such credentials are read via this
engine.
Using the
.Qo Li file: Qc
schema is optional; a plain file (path) name will do.
.El
.Pp
Options specifying keys, like
.Sy -key
and similar, can use the generic OpenSSL engine key loading URI scheme
.Qo Li org.openssl.engine: Qc
to retrieve private keys and public keys.
The URI syntax is as follows, in simplified form:
.Bd -literal
    org.openssl.engine:{engineid}:{keyid}
.Ed
.Pp
Where
.Qo Li {engineid} Qc
is the identity/name of the engine, and
.Qo Li {keyid} Qc
is a key identifier that's acceptable by that engine.
For example, when using an engine that interfaces against a PKCS#11
implementation, the generic key URI would be something like this (this
happens to be an example for the PKCS#11 engine that's part of OpenSC):
.Bd -literal
    -key org.openssl.engine:pkcs11:label_some-private-key
.Ed
.Pp
As a third possibility, for engines and providers that have implemented
their own
.Xr OSSL_STORE_LOADER 3 ,
.Qo Li org.openssl.engine: Qc
should not be necessary.
For a PKCS#11 implementation that has implemented such a loader, the
PKCS#11 URI as defined in RFC 7512 should be possible to use directly:
.Bd -literal
    -key pkcs11:object=some-private-key;pin-value=1234
.Ed
.Ss Provider Options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -provider Em name
Load and initialize the provider identified by
.Em name .
The
.Em name
can be also a path to the provider module.
In that case the provider name will be the specified path and not just
the provider module name.
Interpretation of relative paths is platform specific.
The configured "MODULESDIR" path,
.Sy OPENSSL_MODULES
environment variable, or the path specified by
.Sy -provider-path
is prepended to relative paths.
See
.Xr provider 7
for a more detailed description.
.It Sy -provider-path Em path
Specifies the search path that is to be used for looking for providers.
Equivalently, the
.Sy OPENSSL_MODULES
environment variable may be set.
.It Sy -provparam Em [name:]key=value
Set configuration parameter
.Em key
to value
.Em val
in provider
.Em name
(optional), if
.Em name
is not specified, the setting will be applied to all loaded providers.
This option can be specified multiple times, to set multiple parameters.
Options that specify nondefault providers to load should precede this
option if the setting is intended to apply to the to be loaded
providers.
Parameters that only affect provider initialisation must, for now, be
set in the configuration file, only parameters that are also queried as
needed later have any affect when set via this interface.
Only UTF8-string-valued parameters are supported.
See the documentation of the specific provider and associated algorithms
for any supported parameters.
.It Sy -propquery Em propq
Specifies the
.Em property query clause
to be used when fetching algorithms from the loaded providers.
See
.Xr property 7
for a more detailed description.
.El
